import{_ as a,P as i,d as n,e as s,y as o,q as r,f as c}from"./chunks/framework.D7CAgLCq.js";const y=JSON.parse(`{"title":"D.01 The Framework Itself, Explanatory Tasks, and Borrowing From Theory","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"D.01 The Framework Itself, Explanatory Tasks, and Borrowing From Theory","xkChars":0,"xkWords":512,"xkLessons":{"D.01":{"title":"D.01 The framework itself, explanatory tasks, and theoretical borrowing","lead":"The same automated reminder system may reduce missed tasks without making appeals usable. Separate the outcomes to be explained first, so we can identify materials needed for technology, institutions, and people’s experiences, and determine what a borrowed theory actually contributes.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 4980–4995; original anchor section-d-01. Short quotation translated from Chinese: “First ask which outcome needs explanation, rather than first selecting the vocabulary one knows best.” The source excerpt for this lesson is separately attached verbatim in source-slice.md; explanation and original text are distinct.","D.01 makes the explanatory task itself an object of inquiry. Why equipment fails, how decisions form, who bears consequences, and how people understand experiences are adjacent but distinct questions. Technical mechanisms, institutional authority, and actor experience may correct one another or complete separate local explanations. State outcomes and tasks before selecting materials and methods, without granting permanent priority to structure, interests, power, psychology, culture, or technology on reputation alone. Main-text §§1.3–1.10 particularly emphasize that knowledge need not first be fully converted into structural variables, and understanding itself can be an endpoint. [source theory-cross]","Theoretical borrowing has at least five statuses: observational heuristics help discover questions; definitions specify word use; testable mechanisms make fallible claims about actual processes; formal inferences hold under explicit mathematical premises; normative choices express what should be protected and how to weigh tradeoffs. They cannot serve as evidence for one another. Defining a relation as feedback does not prove real feedback effective; a mathematical structure’s validity does not prove that real objects meet its assumptions; effective factual causation cannot leap to a duty to adopt. Main-text §§2.2–2.7 specify responsibilities for classification, causation, evidence, analogy, and sources. [source theory-cross]","A formal mapping should specify the relations preserved, distinctions lost, and circumstances that could change the conclusion. The same word in two theories may refer to different objects, variables, or inferential permissions, so word-for-word substitution is invalid. If the source domain concerns conservation of quantities while the target domain borrows only a diagram of “flow,” conservation conclusions cannot be carried over; if a mapping suppresses differences in people’s refusal and rights, its concise representation cannot ground action either. When existing domain methods suffice, checking sources and boundaries is enough; additional circles need not be invented merely to display the framework’s involvement.","The original automatic-reminder example is a teaching example, not a real project already investigated. Fewer missed tasks is a technical task metric; whether appeals can change errors concerns procedural remedy; whether people are respected also requires users’ actual circumstances and expressions. These three outcomes may diverge: real improvement in the first should be retained, deficiencies in the second made explicit, and the third kept unresolved when materials are absent. Calling everything “a better system” conceals differences, while negating all achievements because one item falls short also distorts them.","Historical adoption, mention in a directory, subsequently discovered compatibility, and an analyst’s new connection must be kept separately. Actual adoption of a theory in an original work requires the corresponding text and version; a name in a directory establishes only mention; current compatibility cannot retroactively establish past influence; a connection proposed by a researcher today is new analysis, not a rewritten intellectual history of the author. Versions with the same number remain different identities when they belong to different texts; obtaining A’s original cannot supply B’s still-unverified wording. This concretely implements the source and expression boundaries of §§16.15–16.16 in the map.","Passing procedures, complete files, and closed identifier references support engineering correspondence, not semantic or real-world gain. Files can have perfect formatting while misreading concepts; citations can be authentic without supporting the current inference. First check content and argument, then external facts and effects of use according to purpose; a checking tool’s pass signal must not substitute for theoretical correctness. Conversely, engineering checks do have local value: they can detect omissions and formatting errors, but must be named according to their actual capabilities.","The five answer types are conceptual, textual, formal, domain-empirical, and use-effectiveness answers. Conceptual answers clarify terminology; textual answers interpret originals; formal answers complete conditional derivations; domain-empirical answers support specific relationships in the world; use-effectiveness answers compare whether a method actually improves work. They are not a maturity ladder in which absence of a later type cancels everything earlier; yet obtaining the first four cannot supply the fifth. More materials and clearer writing may help, but effects still need checking in actual tasks.","Value comparisons must fix actual treatment contents, tasks, and proportionate resources, retain sufficiently strong professional methods as comparators, and include nondelivery, misjudgment, waiting, and maintenance burdens. If a new framework answers more tasks or uses more materials, those extra resources should also be reported; not every outcome difference can be attributed solely to the theory. Without comparison, honestly retain conceptual and textual achievements without declaring real-world superiority, equivalence, or uselessness.","The eight topic positions can be understood as follows: 01.01 tracks changes in questions and goals; 01.02 distinguishes concepts, metaphors, mechanisms, and theorems; 01.03 separates documented active choices from historical gaps unseen in the materials; 01.04 prevents one interpretation from suppressing domain questions in advance; 01.05 distinguishes the theory itself, application layers, and tool packaging; 01.06 checks boundaries, object identity, and circle construction; 01.07 allows counterexamples, failures, replacement, and exit; 01.08 checks the relationship between the intellectual framework and its Skill engineering implementation. These numbers are research navigation, not a claim here to have obtained eight full original studies.","The strongest opposing position worries that, without a preset priority of explanations, analysis loses direction. The response is that a mechanism may be given primary importance based on the task and existing evidence, without permanent privilege across tasks. Another objection holds that if domain methods can already answer the question, the framework is useless; the more precise conclusion is that no additional representational layer is needed here. The framework’s boundary checking may be light or may step aside; yielding cannot be interpreted as requiring the theory either to prevail or to fail completely."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Primary domain explanation","leftText":"Selected according to the current task and materials.","rightTitle":"Permanent priority","rightText":"Assuming before examining evidence that every problem ultimately reduces to one vocabulary; the original excludes this."},{"leftTitle":"Definition and heuristic","leftText":"Specify terms or propose questions to test.","rightTitle":"Mechanisms and formal conclusions","rightText":"Require respectively empirically testable materials or proof under explicit premises; neither follows from similar terminology."},{"leftTitle":"Textual and conceptual answers","leftText":"May have independently established interpretive value.","rightTitle":"Benefits of real-world use","rightText":"Require actual comparison; clearer writing cannot automatically supply them."},{"leftTitle":"Historical adoption","leftText":"Use documented in corresponding texts and versions.","rightTitle":"Current new connection","rightText":"A relationship proposed now by the analyst; it cannot be backfilled as historical fact."},{"leftTitle":"Engineering closure","leftText":"Files, identifiers, formats, or processes conform to agreements.","rightTitle":"Semantic and empirical correctness","rightText":"Interpretation, evidence, and actual effects must be tested separately."}],"boundaries":["The original automatic-reminder case is a teaching construction; this lesson does not package it as real research.","Obtaining one original cannot fill unverified sentences in another text; identical version numbers do not mean identical objects.","Formal mappings may fail; similar words do not guarantee transfer of mechanisms, conservation results, or normative conclusions.","Unmeasured use effects do not cancel independent textual interpretation, nor permit claims of real-world benefits.","Research entries 01.01–01.08 are explained only through the current original; their identifiers are not used to claim access to all historical studies."],"questions":["Can equipment failure be explained primarily in technical terms?","Why cannot complete files certify semantic correctness?","How should one report conceptual, textual, and formal answers when materials on use effects are still absent?","Can identifiers for research entries in the map establish historical adoption?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-01/figures/section-d-01.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"A real methodological document: accessibility evaluation and user experience have separate evidentiary boundaries","href":"https://www.w3.org/TR/2026/NOTE-wcag-em-2-20260723/"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"A real methodological document: accessibility evaluation and user experience have separate evidentiary boundaries","text":"The WCAG-EM 2.0 Group Note published by W3C on July 23, 2026, states under Relation to WCAG 2 conformance claims that evaluating only some webpages is insufficient for a conformance claim about an entire website; Involving users explains that participation by users with disabilities can help reveal barriers that expert evaluation may miss, and recommends including real users with diverse abilities. This shows that scope limits and materials from people’s actual use have separate responsibilities; one technical check cannot settle every conclusion about use.","sourceLabel":"W3C WCAG-EM 2.0, version of 2026-07-23","sourceUrl":"https://www.w3.org/TR/2026/NOTE-wcag-em-2-20260723/","sourceLocator":"Opening date and Status; Relation to WCAG 2 conformance claims, paragraphs 1–3; Involving users, sole body paragraph","boundary":"Checks only this methodological document’s scope and participation recommendations, not actual compliance of any website, improved experience, or this framework’s historical adoption of the document; the Group Note adds no new WCAG normative requirements.","href":"https://www.w3.org/TR/2026/NOTE-wcag-em-2-20260723/"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"D.01","start_line":5036,"end_line":5051,"startLine":5036,"endLine":5051}],"outputId":"section-d-01","sourceHash":"fcf100a5c8688d1e0337c52750ce3ad21fb5dbcc250f97946602ce094345f825","argumentSteps":["First specify the outcome, object, task, and required answer type, preserving each domain’s independent explanatory responsibilities.","When borrowing theories, mark heuristic, definitional, mechanistic, formal, or normative status, and check which distinctions a formal mapping preserves and loses.","Collect evidence separately for technical improvement, procedural remedy, human experience, and other outcomes; they neither certify nor wholly cancel one another.","Distinguish historical adoption, mention, compatibility, and new connections, checking sources text by text and version by version; engineering passes support only engineering facts.","To evaluate benefits of method use, additionally compare under fixed tasks and resources with strong professional comparators, retaining failures and total burdens."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Expanding the original teaching example: three parallel outcomes","text":"Suppose reminders actually reduce missed tasks, but appeals against erroneous decisions cannot change outcomes. The technical task improves locally while remedy remains deficient; assessing whether users feel respected requires separate materials on their circumstances and expressions. These three cannot be compressed into one score of system quality.","assumptions":"Uses the original’s explicitly identified teaching example; no real institution or effect data are indicated.","boundary":"The local improvement holds only within the assumptions, not as a real-world causal estimate."},{"title":"Teaching construction: mapping a word with different meanings","text":"Suppose an engineering model defines flow as material quantity per unit time, while organizational discussion borrows flow to mean the number of information retellings. Both draw arrows, but this does not permit using material-conservation theorems to infer lossless understanding of information; variables must be defined separately, and duplication and omissions checked.","assumptions":"The two models are teaching illustrations and cite no real engineering parameters.","boundary":"This only limits cross-domain inference; it does not deny formal borrowing where an explicit mathematical mapping exists."},{"title":"Teaching construction: version identity is not a number","text":"Suppose text A is labeled 5.3.1 and tool B is also labeled 5.3.1, but their contents and version systems differ. Correct records give each complete title, object, and version; identical numbers cannot establish that A already contains B’s assertions.","assumptions":"The version example is a teaching construction; in this commission, Skill 5.3.1 and the theory manuscript do need separate identity records.","boundary":"Identical numbers cannot establish a source relationship; actual adoption still requires each text."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Reminders help, so users must be respected and appeals must be effective.","effect":"The three outcomes have different evidentiary responsibilities; technical metrics do not testify for procedures or experience.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Use effects have not been measured, so all completed conceptual explanations and formal proofs are invalid.","effect":"The missing fifth answer type limits only effect conclusions; it does not cancel other independently established answers.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Both texts say feedback, so all causal conclusions can be transplanted directly.","effect":"Objects, variables, mappings, and target-domain materials must be checked; identical words do not guarantee identical permitted inferences.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Can equipment failure be explained primarily in technical terms?","answer":"Yes, if the task and materials support it; the original rejects permanent technical priority across all tasks.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why cannot complete files certify semantic correctness?","answer":"Format correspondence and conceptual understanding are different propositions; even authentic citations may be used for unsupported inferences.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"How should one report conceptual, textual, and formal answers when materials on use effects are still absent?","answer":"Retain the first three achievements, judge separately according to available domain-empirical materials, and state that use benefits remain unestablished, without exaggeration or wholesale cancellation.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Can identifiers for research entries in the map establish historical adoption?","answer":"No. They provide navigation only; the corresponding original versions and actual locations of use must be obtained.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-01/figures/section-d-01.png","alt":"What outcome is being explained? Technical/institutional/experiential questions may each have answers; distinguish borrowing status: heuristic/definition/mechanism, formal/normative; what can a mapping preserve, what distinctions does it lose, and what materials would change the conclusion? Separate adoption/mention/compatibility from the analyst’s new connection; conceptual, textual, formal, and empirical answers cannot automatically supply actual use benefits; compare tasks and total burdens against strong professional comparators, retaining nondelivery and failure. Drawn by the explanatory author following D.01; the original reminder example retains its teaching status, and engineering checks do not certify semantics or real-world benefits.","caption":"Drawn by the explanatory author following D.01; the original reminder example retains its teaching status, and engineering checks do not certify semantics or real-world benefits."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static figure; interaction has not yet been implemented, so the following references to selection, switching, or clicking are only ideas for later design. Switch the outcome being asked about and answer type to display corresponding materials; switch historical-adoption/new-connection labels while preserving source responsibilities.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"A real methodological document illustrates the division of responsibilities between evaluation scope and user materials; this is a bounded connection made by the current analyst, not retrospective confirmation of the theory’s historical adoption."},"openQuestions":["The full original studies indicated by research entries 01.01–01.08 were not all obtained; their entire arguments or adoption histories cannot be reconstructed from the directory.","This lesson has conducted no comparison of actual reader or tool-use effects."],"taskId":"S-D-01","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research draft, full-text lines 4525–4544; see sourceRefs for precise section ranges","claim_support":["intro-20-appendix-d: terminology, contracts, burden of argument, and boundaries of the original text; not certification of empirical facts","D.01: terminology, contracts, burden of argument, and boundaries of the original text; not certification of empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-cross","title":"Historical research draft: Xikari tasks, argument status, and source protocols","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"§1.3–1.10, Chinese source lines 99–181; §2.2–2.7.1, Chinese source lines 239–355","claim_support":["Independence of domain tasks, proposition classification, analogy and source relations, and answer ceilings"],"limits":["Theoretical explanations do not serve as external empirical support.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"w3c-em2","title":"WCAG Evaluation Methodology (WCAG-EM) 2.0","url":"https://www.w3.org/TR/2026/NOTE-wcag-em-2-20260723/","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"W3C Group Note 2026-07-23","locator":"Opening date/Status; Relation to WCAG2 conformance claims, paragraphs 1–3; body paragraph under Involving users","claim_support":["A sample evaluation cannot directly claim whole-site conformance; user involvement helps find barriers experts may miss."],"limits":["An informative Group Note, not an additional standard; it proves neither specific implementation nor historical adoption of this framework."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2.1","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2.1, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2.1 / 2026-11-19","locator":"D.01　框架自身、解释任务与理论借用; Chinese source lines 4980–4995; SHA-256 2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2.1; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-01/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-01/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-01/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-01/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v2.1; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"977fa71b7a56449260514cfc236c1a588cb559f829caae88fa5d2993ada61b85","source_sha256":"2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-01.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-01.md"}`),l={name:"en/theory/appendix-d/d-01.md"};function d(h,e,u,p,m,f){const t=i("SectionLesson");return n(),s("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=o('<div class="theory-source"><h1 id="section-d-01" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">D.01</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>The Framework Itself, Explanatory Tasks, and Borrowing From Theory <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-d-01" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;D.01 The Framework Itself, Explanatory Tasks, and Borrowing From Theory {#section-d-01}&quot;">​</a></h1><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">The same automated reminder system may reduce missed tasks without making appeals usable. Separate the outcomes to be explained first, so we can identify materials needed for technology, institutions, and people’s experiences, and determine what a borrowed theory actually contributes.</span></div><p>In the main text, see §1.3–1.10, §2.2–2.7, and §16.15–16.16. Research entries cover 01.01–01.08.</p><p>Start by asking which outcome needs explaining. Do not begin with whichever vocabulary is most familiar. Why equipment failed, why a decision took shape, who bears the consequences, and how the people concerned understand what they experienced are related but distinct tasks. Technical mechanisms, institutional authority, and lived experience can correct one another or each provide the main account of a particular part of the problem. Structure, interests, power, psychology, culture, and technology should not have permanent priority before evidence is examined.</p><p>Borrowing from theory requires distinguishing at least guidance for observation, definitions, testable mechanisms, formal inferences, and normative choices. The same term appearing in two theories does not guarantee that the objects, variables, or permitted inferences are the same. A formal mapping should state which relation it preserves, which distinctions it loses, and what circumstances would change the conclusion. If a domain method already answers the question sufficiently, the framework can limit its role to checking sources and boundaries. It need not introduce additional circles into the analysis.</p><p>In a teaching example, an automatic reminder reduces missed tasks, but appeals still cannot change errors. Improvement on the technical task can coexist with inadequate procedural remedy. To ask whether users are treated with respect, one also needs evidence of their actual circumstances and what they say. Calling all three outcomes “a better system” would obscure the differences. It would likewise be a mistake to let a shortcoming in one invalidate all the gains.</p><p>Keep historical adoption of a theory, mention in a catalog, compatibility discovered later, and new connections proposed by an analyst separate. Versions in different texts do not have the same identity merely because they share a number. Obtaining one manuscript cannot supply wording in another source that has yet to be checked. Successful procedural checks, complete files, and a closed numbering scheme establish only the corresponding facts about the engineering work. They do not establish that the meanings are correct or that use in reality improves outcomes.</p><p>An analysis can yield answers at the conceptual, textual, formal, domain-empirical, and practical levels in succession. The first four answers, where established, do not disappear because the last has yet to be obtained. Clearer writing or more material cannot stand in for practical benefits. To judge the value of an added method, fix the actual material processed, the task, and comparable resources. Retain a strong domain method as a comparison and account for undelivered work, misjudgments, waiting, and maintenance burden.</p><p>Research topics. 01.01 Original questions and changes in objectives. 01.02 The status of concepts, metaphors, mechanisms, and theorems. 01.03 Deliberate exclusions and gaps in the historical record. 01.04 Explanatory priorities and theoretical colonization. 01.05 The framework itself, its application layer, and its tool layer. 01.06 Boundaries, identity, and construction of circles. 01.07 Counterexamples, failure, replacement, and exit. 01.08 Conceptual frameworks and the engineering of Skill packages.</p></div>',1)),r(t,{section:"D.01"}),e[1]||(e[1]=c("div",{class:"theory-source"},null,-1))])}const b=a(l,[["render",d]]);export{y as __pageData,b as default};
